# SessionVerifier — a concrete async AUTHENTICATION adapter. Declares its port
# (session_verification) — the inverted arrow. Named for its IDENTITY (it verifies a
# session credential), NOT its transport : that it speaks OAuth, checks a signature,
# or calls a provider is a detail of HOW it verifies, configured in .world — never
# the adapter's name. Always async, never locks.
#
# NAMED "session_verification", not "authentication" (parser-removal plan, Phase
# 1a) — hecksagain's own vendored library already ships an `authentication.port`
# (verb authenticated_by, signal :reply : a different, simpler synchronous-check
# concept). Registry#add_port is a flat name-keyed overwrite and load_library runs
# before load_domain in the SAME boot (runtime/loader.rb) — reusing the name
# "authentication" here would silently clobber the library's port with this
# effect-shaped one, or vice versa depending on load order. Distinct name, distinct
# registry slot, no collision.
#
# The bind `AuthIdentity::AuthIdentity.verified_by("SessionVerifier", on: "Established")`
# resolves IFF SessionVerifier's port (session_verification) carries `verified_by`. On
# the verdict it dispatches AuthIdentity.MarkVerified (success) or .Reject (failure)
# — the success/failure branches of the effect. Endpoint, token, and timeout live in
# .world.
Hecks.adapter "SessionVerifier" do
  port "session_verification"
end
